Subject: ef.f: declare LOGICAL LIMSCF in EFSTR

WHAT IT FIXES
  Subroutine EFSTR (ef.f) pulls LIMSCF in through `COMMON /SCFTYP/ EMIN,
  LIMSCF` and then assigns `LIMSCF=.FALSE.`, but -- unlike subroutine EF at
  the top of the same file -- it never declares it LOGICAL, so under the
  default implicit rules LIMSCF is REAL. f2c refuses the assignment with

      Error on line 590 of ef.f: impossible conversion

  and produces a truncated efstr_ (f2c still exits 0, so the build script
  greps its output for "Error on line"). Real compilers of the day accepted
  it because the COMMON block fixed the type. One declaration line restores
  the intent; the generated C is unchanged apart from the type of that one
  variable.

--- a/ef.f
+++ b/ef.f
@@ -562,6 +562,7 @@
      $MODE,NSTEP,NEGREQ,IPRNT
       DIMENSION IPOW(9)               
       LOGICAL RESTRT,SCF1,LDUM,LUPD,log,rrscal,donr,gnmin 
+      LOGICAL LIMSCF
       CHARACTER*241 KEYWRD,LINE                                               
       CHARACTER CHDOT*1,ZERO*1,NINE*1,CH*1
       DATA CHDOT,ZERO,NINE   /'.','0','9'/                     
